-- title:
--   Theorem 1 (Main theorem), pp.1–2 — CCRA-computable ⇔ permutation/affine normal form ⇔ $\mathcal G_f^\circ$ $\mathbb Q$-triangularizable with commuting unipotents ⇔ (C4)
-- statement:
--   Let $f:\Sigma^*\to\mathbb Q$ be an invertible rational series on a finite alphabet, with minimal representation $f(w)=\lambda M_w\gamma$ ($M_a$ invertible $d\times d$ rational matrices), $\Gamma_f\le GL_d(\mathbb Q)$ the group generated by the $M_a$, $\mathcal G_f\subseteq GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ its Zariski closure and $\mathcal G_f^\circ$ its identity component.
--   The following are equivalent.
--
--   1. **(C1)** $f$ is computed by a deterministic finite-state polynomial CCRA over $\mathbb Q$.
--   2. **(C2)** $f$ is computed by a CCRA whose control is a permutation automaton, in which every register is updated in isolation by a map $x\mapsto\alpha x+\beta$ with $\alpha\in\mathbb Q^\times$, $\beta\in\mathbb Q$, and whose outputs are linear combinations of products of distinct registers.
--   3. **(C3)** $\mathcal G_f^\circ$ is triangularizable over $\mathbb Q$, and any two unipotent elements of $\mathcal G_f$ commute.
--   4. **(C4)** $\Gamma_f$ has a subgroup of finite index that is triangularizable over $\mathbb Q$ and in which any two elements have commuting unipotent Jordan parts.
--
--   Moreover, every function computed by a machine as in (C2) is invertible.
--
--   This is the first exact characterization of CCRA-computability on a nontrivial class of rational series: an intrinsic, group-theoretic test on the minimal representation together with an explicit normal-form machine.
--
--   **Formalization Note** In (C1) the machines are unrestricted CCRA: any finite control, any number of registers, rational constants, and updates that may reset, merge or multiply registers. The alphabet is any finite type; $d=0$ is allowed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: condition on "the" minimal representation of $f$ → hypotheses: $(\lambda,M,\gamma)$ is any minimal representation of $f$ with invertible letter matrices; (C3), (C4) are stated for its $M$; reason: minimal representations are unique up to simultaneous conjugation and (C3), (C4) are conjugation invariant (p.2); same instances
--   - encoding: $f$ is invertible: its minimal letter matrices are invertible → $f$ has some minimal representation with invertible letter matrices; reason: minimal representations exist for rational series and are unique up to conjugation
--   - encoding: copyless polynomial expressions; (C2)'s isolated updates and output shape → syntax trees with an occurrence multiset (copyless = no repeated occurrence across the tuple); (C2)'s update and output conditions stated on the functions the expressions compute; reason: copylessness is a property of the written expression; a function of the form $\alpha x_i+\beta$ is computed by the literal expression
--   - encoding: Zariski closure via polynomials in the entries and $\det^{-1}$ → polynomials in the entries, closed sets taken relative to $GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$; reason: multiplying by a power of $\det$, a unit on $GL$, gives the same closed subsets of $GL$
--   - encoding: identity component $\mathcal G^\circ$ of the algebraic group → the union of all Zariski-connected subsets of $\mathcal G$ containing $1$ (the connected component of $1$); reason: this is the identity component of a linear algebraic group; its group and finite-index properties (A1) are theorems, not definitions
--   - encoding: the unipotent Jordan part $g_u$ → a relation: $u$ is unipotent, commutes with a semisimple $s$, and $g=su$; reason: the Jordan decomposition is unique, so for invertible $g$ exactly one $u$ qualifies
-- source:
--   Zeng–Cao–Dai–Wang–Ding, Copyless Cost Register Automata and Algebraic Groups, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 434d3094df2665371c402d5b5291b3c7f3a27a0d79384e4a8ec3ca3a46a92543), Theorem 1, pp.1–2 (PDF pp.3–4)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_CCRA
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_IsRep
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_zariskiClosure

namespace CopylessCRA

/-- **Theorem 1 (Main theorem).** For an invertible rational series `f : Σ* → ℚ` the following are
equivalent: (C1) `f` is computed by a deterministic finite-state polynomial CCRA over `ℚ`; (C2) `f`
is computed by a CCRA whose control is a permutation automaton, in which every register is updated
in isolation by a map `x ↦ αx + β` with `α ∈ ℚ×`, `β ∈ ℚ`, and whose outputs are linear combinations
of products of distinct registers; (C3) `𝒢°_f` is triangularizable over `ℚ`, and any two unipotent
elements of `𝒢_f` commute; (C4) `Γ_f` has a subgroup of finite index that is triangularizable over
`ℚ` and in which any two elements have commuting unipotent Jordan parts. Moreover, every function
computed by a machine as in (C2) is invertible.

Zeng–Cao–Dai–Wang–Ding, *Copyless Cost Register Automata and Algebraic Groups*, STOC 2027 submission
(manuscript sha256 434d3094…), Theorem 1, pp.1–2 (PDF pp.3–4).

**Formalization Note** "The minimal representation" is any minimal representation
`(λ, M, γ)` of `f` with invertible letter matrices; (C3) and (C4) are stated for its `M`
(minimal representations are unique up to simultaneous conjugation, and (C3), (C4) are invariant
under it). Words are lists, `M_w = M_{a₁}⋯M_{aₙ}`, `f(w) = λ M_w γ`. The alphabet is any finite type
and `d = 0` is allowed. `𝒢_f` is the Zariski closure of `Γ_f` in `GL_d(ℚ̄)` and `𝒢°_f` its
Zariski-connected component of `1`. -/
theorem theorem_1 {α : Type*} [Fintype α] :
    (∀ (f : List α → ℚ) (d : ℕ) (lam : Fin d → ℚ) (M : α → Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℚ)
        (γ : Fin d → ℚ), IsRep f lam M γ → IsMinimalRep lam M γ → (∀ a, IsUnit (M a)) →
        List.TFAE [C1 f, C2 f, C3 M, C4 M]) ∧
    ∀ f : List α → ℚ, C2 f → IsInvertibleSeries f := by sorry

end CopylessCRA
